---
const layers = [
  { number: '60%', name: 'The structure', line: 'What we build for your company: your templates, your rules, your shared files and the steps each job follows.' },
  { number: '30%', name: 'The handoffs', line: 'How the work moves between your people: who brings what, who checks it and who signs off.' },
  { number: '10%', name: 'The AI', line: 'The AI does its part inside that structure, with the right files and clear steps in front of it.' },
];

const teaching = [
  { name: 'See it.', line: 'I show your people the whole job working, start to finish.' },
  { name: 'Do it.', line: 'They try it with me beside them.' },
  { name: 'Make it yours.', line: 'They use it on their own work, and I correct what they do.' },
  { name: 'Take it somewhere new.', line: 'They use the same steps on a different job.' },
];

const limits = [
  'EdubaWare is an early-stage platform. Its screens and features will change.',
  'Anyone in a workspace can change or delete a file, and there is no undo.',
  'If two people edit the same thing at once, one edit can overwrite the other.',
  'There are no scheduled runs. Nothing runs until someone sends a message.',
  'Everyone in a workspace can read and download every file in it.',
  'Microsoft 365 and SharePoint connections are not ready.',
  'Each outside app you connect uses one login for the whole team, not one for each person.',
  'Your company\'s EdubaWare managers and I can see every chat, and we are there to assist.',
];
---

<section class="container way" id="how">
  <h2>The AI is the smallest part</h2>
  <p class="intro">
    Taping AI onto a messy process gets you a faster mess. I build the structure first, then add the AI.
    My 60/30/10 rule shows where the value sits, and why the AI can do its job.
  </p>
  <div class="layers">
    {
      layers.map((layer) => (
        <article class="teams-card depth-box">
          <p class="number">{layer.number}</p>
          <h3>{layer.name}</h3>
          <p>{layer.line}</p>
        </article>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <p class="closer">Drop AI into a mess and it mostly guesses. Give it structure and it has something to work with.</p>

  <div class="columns">
    <article class="teams-card depth-box">
      <h3>How I teach</h3>
      <p>Jake teaches at scale. I teach one company at a time, inside your real work. I have been teaching for over 24 years. This is how I teach:</p>
      <ol>{teaching.map((step) => <li><strong>{step.name}</strong> {step.line}</li>)}</ol>
      <p class="partner">I teach your people to use AI as a thought partner. Brief it the way you would brief a new coworker, with what is happening, why it matters and what you need back.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="teams-card depth-box" id="limits">
      <h3>Know this before you start</h3>
      <ul>{limits.map((limit) => <li>{limit}</li>)}</ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .way {
    padding-top: var(--space-7);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-7);
  }

  h2 {
    color: var(--edubaware);
  }

  .layers {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-5);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .closer {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .partner {
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
  }

  .number {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-1);
    color: var(--edubaware);
    font-size: 2rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.1;
  }

  .layers p:last-child {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  .columns {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-5);
  }

  .columns p,
  ul,
  ol {
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
  }

  ul,
  ol {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: var(--space-5);
  }

  li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
  }

  li:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 980px) {
    .layers,
    .columns {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
